The mvn deploy command is used in Maven to deploy your project's artifacts (like JARs, WARs, etc.) to a remote repository.
This is typically done after building your project and is often used in a continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipeline.

Here’s a basic example of how to use the mvn deploy command:

bash

Verify

Open In Editor
Run
Copy code
mvn clean deploy
Explanation:
mvn: This is the command to run Maven.
clean: This phase cleans the target directory, removing any previously compiled artifacts.
deploy: This phase packages the project and deploys the artifact to the remote repository specified in your pom.xml file.
Example pom.xml Configuration:
To successfully deploy, your pom.xml should have the necessary configuration for the distribution management section, which specifies the remote repository where the artifacts will be deployed.
Here’s a simple example:

xml

Verify

Open In Editor
Run
Copy code
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>my-app</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <distributionManagement>
        <repository>
            <id>my-repo</id>
            <url>http://my.repository.url/repo</url>
        </repository>
    </distributionManagement>
</project>
Authentication:
If your remote repository requires authentication, you can configure your credentials in the settings.xml file located in the .m2 directory (usually found in your home directory).
Here’s an example of how to set that up:

xml

